/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A circuit is planar when you can draw it on paper with no branch crossing another.
   - Give each mesh a clockwise current i₁, i₂, and so on.
   - Why it works: KCL is satisfied automatically, because each mesh current enters and leaves every node it passes.
   - Together the equations form R i = v.
   - Dependent sources.
   - A current source fixes a current, but its voltage is set by the rest of the circuit, so a KVL equation through it would carry a new unknown.
   - Sign rule for the constraint: compare the source arrow with each mesh current where it passes through the shared branch.
   - The same steps work for AC circuits with impedances in place of resistances (see applying network theorems to AC circuits).
*/
